<section class="section section-tight">
  <div class="container two-col-grid">
    <div class="content-panel">
      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">Tech vs. traditional operations — the comp gap</h2>
      <p>Operations is one of the highest-leverage career moves for professionals who have built operational excellence in traditional industries and are targeting tech companies. A Director of Operations at a Series B tech company earns $200K–$320K total comp. The same director at a logistics or manufacturing company earns $130K–$210K. The $70K–$110K gap is almost entirely equity — tech companies grant RSUs to operations leaders that traditional employers do not.</p>
      <p>The positioning challenge for operations professionals moving into tech is making scale, systems thinking, and measurable operational improvements read clearly for tech hiring managers who are calibrating for "does this person understand building and scaling operations from the inside." The experience transfers; the framing often does not without deliberate work.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-panel">
      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">Operations offer negotiation</h2>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Quantify operational impact</strong> — Cost per unit reduced, throughput increased, error rate lowered, headcount scaled without proportional cost increase — lead with these numbers</li>
        <li><strong>Equity at Director and above</strong> — RSU grants are standard at tech companies at the Director level and above; negotiate the grant and refresh explicitly</li>
        <li><strong>Scale as leverage</strong> — "Managed 50 people" is not leverage. "Built the operational system that scaled from 50 to 300 without a proportional increase in unit cost" is</li>
        <li><strong>Benchmark against tech operations comp</strong> — Even if transitioning from traditional industry, tech-company operations benchmarks are the right reference when targeting a move</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
