    <div class="faq-header">
      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">Operations manager salary — common questions</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-grid">
      <details class="faq-card" open>
        <summary>What is a good operations manager salary in 2026?</summary>
        <p>A good total compensation for an Operations Manager at a tech or high-growth company is $110K–$175K. At a financial services firm, $95K–$155K. At a manufacturing or logistics company, $75K–$120K. For a Director of Operations at a tech company, $200K–$320K total comp. Tech-company operations leaders earn 30–60% more than traditional industry peers because equity grants create a total comp premium that base salary comparisons do not capture.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-card">
        <summary>What does a VP of Operations earn in 2026?</summary>
        <p>A VP of Operations at a tech or high-growth company earns $265K–$450K total comp in 2026, including base, bonus, and equity. At financial services firms, $215K–$360K. At manufacturing or logistics companies, $165K–$275K. At the VP level, equity grants are typically $80K–$200K annualized at tech companies — making equity negotiation as important as base salary negotiation.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-card">
        <summary>How do operations managers negotiate a higher salary?</summary>
        <p>Three specific levers: (1) Quantify operational impact — cost per unit reduced, throughput improved, headcount scaled without proportional cost increase are the numbers that move negotiations. (2) Equity — at Director and VP level, RSU grants are negotiable and often left on the table by candidates from non-tech backgrounds who are not expecting the equity conversation. (3) Competing offer — a single competing offer from a comparable tech or high-growth company is the most reliable way to close the gap. See the full <a href="/how-to-negotiate-salary/">salary negotiation guide</a> for scripts.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-card">
        <summary>When should an operations professional hire a career coach?</summary>
        <p>Four high-ROI situations: (1) Moving from traditional industry operations into a tech company — the positioning must shift from efficiency execution to systems thinking and scale. (2) Targeting a Director or VP role for the first time — the story must shift from managing processes to building operational leverage. (3) Offer in hand from a tech company where equity negotiation is new. (4) Scope has grown significantly but title and compensation have not kept pace. Career coaching at Askia averages a $47K salary increase in compensation-focused engagements.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

